afirewall (20260816.0.0-2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Depend on iproute2. afirewall runs `ip route get` to discover the external
    interface and address, and exits if `ip` is not executable - so on a host
    without iproute2 the package installed cleanly and then refused to do
    anything. iproute2 is priority important and present on any normal Debian
    system, which is why this was never seen; it was found by installing the
    package into a minimal container and running it.
  * No change to the source. This is a packaging fix and carries a Debian
    revision rather than an invented upstream version, which is what the
    non-native format from 20260816.0.0-1 makes possible.

 -- Flattop5377 <Flattop5377@proton.me>  Sun, 16 Aug 2026 09:31:58 -0600

afirewall (20260816.0.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The IPv6 ruleset failed to load entirely on any host with inbound.tcp8000
    enabled: the template matched on `ip saddr` inside an ip6 table, and nft
    refuses a whole table containing one unloadable rule. Such a host came up
    with no IPv6 firewall while its IPv4 ruleset loaded and looked correct.
  * New command `afirewall add-service`, which writes a rules template for a
    service the package does not ship - both address families, the config key,
    the include, the jump, and the reply path in the opposite table. It asks for
    the limit's posture and the reason for it, and refuses without them.
  * New templates: postgres, and udp51821 for a second WireGuard instance on a
    non-default port.
  * The default configuration is now also installed to
    /usr/share/afirewall/afirewall.conf, so a configuration manager can restore
    a known state at the start of a run and let each role add what it needs.
    /etc/afirewall/afirewall.conf remains the conffile and is unchanged.
  * Every rule carrying a rate or connection limit now records, beside it,
    whether that limit refuses excess or only counts it, and why. Inbound
    bitcoin changes from counting to refusing, to agree with the argument its
    own note makes and with the tor relay rules beside it.
  * ICMP: path MTU discovery is carried above the rate limit in IPv4, as it
    already was in IPv6, so a black-holed connection is not competing with
    diagnostics for a budget. Source quench is no longer accepted (RFC 6633
    deprecated it), nor are the timestamp, information and address-mask
    requests.
  * Comments describing one deployment's own network have been removed from the
    templates. The arguments they carried are unchanged; they are now made from
    the shape of the service rather than from one site's use of it.
  * The package is no longer Debian-native, so packaging fixes can be released
    without inventing an upstream version. It now declares nftables (>= 1.0.2),
    which is the oldest version measured to parse a full generated ruleset;
    0.9.8 and earlier reject it.

 -- Flattop5377 <Flattop5377@proton.me>  Sun, 16 Aug 2026 09:29:14 -0600

